! NEXT VECTOR — the plane roster, and the three answers it gives

''THE MEMORY SENSORIUM'S PLANE ROSTER DISAGREES WITH ITSELF, THREE WAYS.'' Measured 2026-08-29 against
a live node:

|! source |! what it names |
| `manifest.json` `has` | content · structure · form · persistence · worldline — FIVE |
| the `sense` door's `--lens` help | content · structure · form · persistence — FOUR |
| the daemon, asked | content (2,843) · structure (2,448) · form (0) — THREE answer, `persistence` refuses |

''Three states wear two answers.'' A plane can be DECLARED in the manifest, COMPOSED on disk, and FED by
a pour, and those are independent: `form` stands declared and composed and empty, `persistence` stands
declared and never composed. Both leave an instrument reading nothing, and only one of them names a
sensorium that is merely young. ''An unfed plane and an absent one must not answer alike'' — a reader
who cannot tell them apart cannot tell a quiet corpus from a broken door.

''The roster is the design's, not the code's.'' It reads as the seam between a plane-set that settled in
the operator's hand and one that settled on disk, so the fix is a RULING before it is a patch: which
planes does a memory sensorium hold, what composes them, and what does a door say about one declared and
unbuilt. `plane_base.PLANE_BASE` is the map to read it off, never memory.

''Held as the next bearing by operator ruling, 2026-08-29.'' The tending rite (`sensorium-runbook`
#surface-drift) carries the surrounding law; this names the one gap it cannot close by walking.

