!! Burr ledger — live only; each carries its wake condition

* ✓ ''INGEST tests RESOLVED 2026-06-14'' (was 4 RED) — root cause = FIXTURE DRIFT, not a gate regression. The 4 vectors mutated the boot via `.replace("# Entry - Lararium Cold Boot", …)`, but the recut changed the heading to `# Entry ~ Lararium Boot`; the silent no-op replace left disk==synced==canonical → noop. Fixed the 5 stale literals + added `.not.toBe(source)` guards so heading drift now fails LOUD at the mutation (the passing fixtures already had the guard; the failing ones lacked it — that was the hole). LESSON: a fixture that mutates the live boot MUST assert the mutation took. Gate logic untouched.
* ''OPERATOR CONCESSION OWED — the realm idle clock''. `BagStowage` cools a bag to `anu` after `idleMs` (default 5 min) via a LOCAL `Date.now()`, and an unfed cooling now reads `dissolved`. So a realm's liveness rides a local wall clock, which `lamplighters` bans: //a logical epoch cannot accrue silence at all// — a per-writer max-register moves only when somebody writes, and //too long untended// names a DURATION. The COOLING CAUSE split (''reclaimed'' vs ''unfed'') landed and closes the resource half; the clock half needs the ruling `lamplighters` already framed: ''(a)'' relax to //no LOCAL wall-clock duration// and stand Roughtime (a repo-wide grep returns ONE hit — it does not stand today), ''(b)'' hold the ban absolute and let a kahu quorum NOTICE, humans as the clock, ''(c)'' a roster — REFUSED by standing ruling. ⚠ Until this is picked, do NOT build the three-state `ahi kā` erosion reading: the middle state (`ahi tere`, guttering) IS a duration and cannot be expressed on the clockless substrate. Wakes on the operator's pick.
* ✓ ''THE RELATION READS FROM BOTH SIDES — LANDED 2026-08-31, walked in docker''. A joining operator read `seed` after a completed crossing because the phase counted only operators SHE had admitted, and a joiner admits nobody. ''Each side holds its own evidence'': the founder's is the admit on her members board; the joiner's is the contract-in she signed. `accept-carriage` now KEEPS that consent beside the charter, EPOCH-BOUND (a consent rooted behind the standing epoch grants nothing), and `nexusPhase` takes `contractedInto` — relations ADD from both directions, and neither count is the roster: no vessel sees the whole. ⚠ ''A draft chased this by pointing the joiner at the founder's members board, and that was an AUTHORITY DEFECT'' — reverted the same session. That board is the Kapae-antigen's ALLOW-twin governing whom THIS vessel blind-transits for; folding a partner's would let their FUTURE admits widen this vessel's carriage, and an operator consents at ONE epoch, never to every admit after. ''The immune plane carries no global roster BY DESIGN'' (no list of devices or users to approve against; the daemon reads BEHAVIOUR). ⚠ THREE PLANES, DO NOT CONFLATE: WHERE (meshpalace/carriage) · WHO (`crossroads`/`oracle` announce — read-open, self-certifying cards, NO write-ACL, so sharing it grants nothing) · IMMUNE (antigen + members, vessel-local, behavioural). STILL OPEN on the WHO plane: cabal heraldry and public Handles publish there via `who-face`/`handle-announce`, and each vessel scopes its board by its OWN key, so two operators hold two boards.
* ''THE SYNC GUARD STANDS UNWIRED — narrow teeth, measured''. `BagStowage.setSyncActive` has NO production caller: `syncActive` is written only at its own setter and read only for display (`lares residency`, the TW5 projection). So the guard in `cool()` and `sweepOnce()` — the automerge-repo#358 invariant //don't evict while another vessel is actively replicating to us// — can never fire. ⚠ Two collides SHRANK this from the shape it first read as: for ''bag'' grains no handle is dropped at all (//until automerge-repo#358 lands a public eviction API the actual bag handle drop stays a TODO inside the hook impl//), so the dead guard costs nothing today; for ''wiki'' grains `unmountWiki` runs a teardown HANDSHAKE (`mkTeardown` → `teardown:ack`) that gives the island its flush, so the exposure narrows to a FORCED terminate after handshake timeout while replication is in flight. Wakes when #358 lands the eviction API, or when a handshake-timeout force-terminate is measured losing work.
* ⚠ ''DO NOT CROSS-WIRE RESIDENCY TO REPLICATION.'' The tempting cure — warm a bag when a remote change arrives — would light the guard AND make `unfed` see the collective, and it is WRONG both times: a remote face's offering would then read as THIS vessel's dwelling. The axes stay split — residency measures LOCAL acts and answers //am I attending//; `realmStanding` reads the per-writer epoch lease slots, which replicate, and answers //who feeds this realm//. A gate needing a fact about the realm reads the slots.
* ⚠ ''THE REALM DOES NOT CROSS OPERATORS — MEASURED IN DOCKER, TWICE, CLEAN''. Two contracted operators fed ONE realm through a live relay; A counted her own two faces and never B's (`maintainerCount:2`, `standing:"visit"`→`"many-faces"` from A''s own pair alone). ''Structural, never a sync delay'': `realm-feed` rolls a slot under `bags/daemon/lease-epoch/{resource}/{writer}`, and `daemonDocUrlFromBootstrap` reads that bag''s URL //off the social bootstrap THIS VESSEL already holds//. ''ONE MECHANISM, TWO PURPOSES, INCOMPATIBLE SCOPES'': `epoch-lease` was built as //the non-renewal half of revocation// — a capability-staling FENCE, vessel-local by nature and correctly homed — and `realm-clock` reuses it as a MAINTENANCE LEDGER, which is shared by nature. A realm is a COLLECTIVE BOUND BY INTERACTION and the record of that interaction sits in each participant''s private drawer, so ''no instrument in the house can currently see a realm''. ⚠ This RETRACTS the reading that `realmStanding` sees the collective because its slots replicate — they do not cross operators. Walked by `tools/mesh-scenarios.sh realm-crossing`, which reports it as a GAP with its wake condition. ⚠ ''AND THE OBVIOUS CURE IS NOT A RELOCATION.'' `realm-feed` takes `writer` as a PLAIN STRING — lowercased, written straight to a slot as `authority: "lares-verb"`, with no signature and no custody proof; canon already states it (//the daemon cannot re-verify from its side that the caller custodies that root//). The CLI derives honestly (`actingFace` checks `held.includes(index)` and loads the real verifying key), so ''the containment today is the socket boundary plus the PRIVATE bag, never a check''. `epoch-lease` argues //last-writer-wins WITHIN a slot is safe — one writer holds it//, and that argument RESTS on the bag being private. Move the ledger to a doc every dweller can write and any dweller could roll any other's slot. Wakes when the ledger moves to the realm SUBSTRATE (`CabalRealm.substrateUrl`) ''WITH a signed write path'' — the fence STAYS in the daemon bag.
* ''NINE SCENARIOS, NO MESH — now ten''. Every scenario before `realm-crossing` stood its hearths peerless (`LAR_A_PEERS=` plus `--no-deps`) and none brought up `herm-source`, so the spread walked nine ISOLATED vessels with hand-carried artifacts and never once walked replication between two operators. `realm-crossing` is the only scenario that stands a relay — and standing one immediately surfaced a second thing nothing had met: ''`restart: "on-failure:8"` does NOT ride the cold-mesh race''. A hearth booting beside a COLD relay exits `reason: 'resolve-timeout'` out of `openDaemon`, and lararium-a exhausted all eight retries while lararium-b, starting moments later against a now-warm relay, stood. The compose note prices the race as //a moment rather than this vessel//; eight retries did not outlast the moment. The scenario stages the relay FIRST and waits for it to ANSWER, so a reading about a realm never turns on boot order. Coverage now reads ''every reachable cell walked'' — 18 cells, 12 claimed, 6 unreachable (the quorum phase needs a third operator the harness does not stand).
* ⚠ ''BOOT PATIENCE IS GRADED BY DISTANCE, AND THE DELAY THAT BITES IS LOAD''. `SCALE_PATIENCE_MS` grants `vessel: 3_000` … `dreamnet: 90_000` on the stated reasoning that //a wider scale traverses more of the mesh, so a transient UNAVAILABLE is more expected//. `@daemon` is a hearth-private doc at VESSEL scale, so it gets 3s — and what actually delays it is two hearths booting into one relay and QUEUEING, which scale does not model. Measured: with the relay already up AND answering, lararium-a died `reason: 'resolve-timeout'` on 8 of 8 restarts while lararium-b stood; the error text prescribes the cure it cannot perform — //Stand again; a vessel under load from its peers commonly resolves on a second reading//. ⚠ ''This is a strong candidate for WHY nine scenarios ran peerless'' — the spread may have been avoiding this flake rather than choosing isolation. `realm-crossing` boots the hearths in SEQUENCE (relay → A → B) to remove the mutual load. ⚠ ''AND THE LOAD NEED NOT COME FROM THE MESH.'' Measured again: running vitest suites on the host WHILE a scenario stood killed `herm-source` itself — 8 of 8 restarts, same `@daemon … within 3000ms`. The relay had stood minutes earlier on the same image and the same volumes; the only change was CPU pressure from an unrelated process. ''A boot that a test suite can starve reads as a code regression'', and it nearly did here — the rebuild that preceded it was the obvious suspect and was innocent. NEVER run suites beside a mesh scenario, and price the 3s as a machine-wide budget rather than a mesh one. ⚠ ''AND THE FOURTH MEASUREMENT BREAKS THE LOAD STORY OPEN — a vessel cannot resolve its OWN just-seeded doc''. On a QUIET box (load 0.51) `herm-source` founded cleanly — keypair, pre-rotation inception, `DaemonDoc seeded url=automerge:3HcfV2df…`, ContactCard, bootstrap written — then died 8 of 8 restarts on //@daemon (automerge:3HcfV2df…) did not resolve within 3000ms//: ''the same doc id it had just written, with no peer involved and nothing else running''. So the 3s vessel patience races a COLD FOUNDING's own storage flush, with keyhive init and identity work sharing the window. ''Not distance, and not only load — cold-start work on the local path alone exhausts it.'' ⚠ This BLOCKS the mesh grid: the quorum scenario stands three hearths and each cold-founds. Wakes with a load/cold term on the patience, or a resolve that waits on the write it just made. ⚠ MEASURED A THIRD TIME, from a process this hand never started: a `lararium-sensorium` python holder at 99% CPU starved `herm-source` through the same 3s window, load average ~2 on an otherwise idle box. ''A mesh scenario is therefore not reproducible on a shared workstation'' — check `ps -eo pcpu` BEFORE believing any boot failure, and never read one as a code regression until the box is quiet. Wakes when patience takes a LOAD term, or when a peered cold boot is measured standing without sequencing.
* ''ONE PARAMETER, THREE PLANES, THREE CORRECT SCOPES — operator's call on the wiring''. `nexusPubkey` reaches six call sites in `open-node-vessel` and every one passes `vesselIdentity.verifyingKey`, while each board's own doc says the address is per-ISLAND (//every island member resolves the one board with no mint-race//). Within one operator's fleet that holds; across two contracted operators each resolves a board only it can see. ''The scopes differ BY PLANE and a blanket change would repeat the reverted authority defect'':
** ''WHO'' (`who-face`) — SHARE. No write-ACL, cards self-certifying; reading grants nothing.
** ''ANTIGEN / DENY'' — SHARE. Quorum-signed against the charter roster, an unverifiable entry is IGNORED, and a foreign ban only TIGHTENS. Its own doc already asks for this: //the immune system saturates the connected mesh//. ⚠ Changes immune behaviour ACROSS operators, so it reads as a governance call rather than a wiring one.
** ''MEMBERS / ALLOW'' — KEEP LOCAL, on CONSENT rather than forgery. ⚠ A draft called these //unsigned local admits// and that reads FALSE: `carriageEntryCounts` requires ≥k kahu quorum signatures AND the operator's contract-in, and `membership-doctrine:95` says //≥k kahu counter-sign it against the charter epoch//. A partner's board VERIFIES. The objection is that an admit only ever WIDENS whom a vessel carries for, and folding a partner's board accepts every admit their quorum makes AFTER the epoch this vessel consented at.
** ✓ ''RULED (operator, 2026-08-31): MEMBERS ARE SHARED.'' //Shared stamps/contracts into the Cabal — or Nexus, or anywhere else a public Handle over a private Persona group gets used to sign — get shared. A human's base veil id keys, and the PersonaGroups they consist of, stay private.// So `persona-circle:189` reads correct in listing MEMBERS among the per-Nexus shared boards, and the earlier vessel-local ruling was scoped wrong. ⚠ ''THE ERROR UNDER IT: a RECORD was mistaken for a POLICY.'' The board records WHO STAMPED IN — a public, signed, verifiable fact about the Cabal. Whether THIS vessel carries for a given member stays this vessel's own reading OVER that shared record. Sharing the record conscripts nobody; only fusing record and decision would.
** ⚠ ''AND THE CHARTER ALREADY HANGS THE HOUSEHOLD — measured, and it outranks the board.'' `seal seat` seats `generateOrLoadPersonaGroupRoot(...).verifyingKey` — the PersonaGroup ROOT — and the charter is the artifact DESIGNED to travel (//public material: seated keys, threshold, epoch lineage//, carried before a partner can consent). Canon already names the compounding: //a founding seat draws every key from ONE vault … three seated chairs at genesis are therefore three faces of one operator//. So a charter publishes ''three PersonaGroup roots of one human, correlated by standing together''. ⚠ Re-keying chairs to Handles moves `keySetHash` → the epoch CID → ''a RE-FOUND'', which is a pre-founding act and this is pre-founding. `signerClass` (landed, tested, UNWIRED) reads a key's class from material a vessel holds and refuses to guess about one it did not mint. Wakes with the stamp re-key — ''one change, two sites'', since re-keying stamps alone leaves the charter leaking.
** ⚠ ''AND RE-KEYING TO A BARE HANDLE IS THE TRAP.'' A Handle is MEANT to be seen and recognition needs it STABLE — which correlates a human across every circle it stands in. ✓ ''The floor already exists and stands UNWIRED'': `deriveCircleScopedKey` extends the tree one level to `m/handle'/context'/circle-scope'`, scope index = HMAC(circleDocId), so //the SAME persona presents a DIFFERENT key to each circle it joins// and //same circle → same leaf// (rejoin-stable). ''No production caller.'' So the re-key wants the SCOPED LEAF, never the bare Handle: a stamp re-keyed to a Handle drops the device-overlay and keeps the cross-Cabal link — a fix shaped like one. `signerClass` now reads the class AND `crossCircleLinkable`, so the trap cannot be walked into silently.
** ⚠ ''AND THE BOARD CANNOT BE SHARED AS IT STANDS — it signs with the PRIVATE layer.'' A membership stamp's nym is `generateOrLoadPersonaGroupRoot(...).verifyingKey`, the PersonaGroup ROOT. A HandleCard's nym is `veiled.verifyingKey` — the veiled-user key, //the sovereign pseudonym presented through the veil//. ''These are different keys, and only the second is the public Handle.'' Publishing the board as-built would put a human's PersonaGroup root — the overlay binding THEIR OWN DEVICES — on every Cabal they stamp into, ''correlating one human's memberships across Nexuses by a key that also identifies their device-group''. The cure is reachable: `deriveVeiledUserKey(seed, handleIndex, contextIndex)` already derives the public layer from the same seed. ⚠ It re-keys what a quorum admits, so it reads as an IDENTITY-PLANE act rather than a wiring one. Wakes on the operator's word to re-key the stamp.
* ''THE ISLAND'S NAME IS ALREADY MINTED'' — `nexusIdentity` (landed, tested) scopes by `genesisSealEpochCid`: content-addressed over the seated key-set and threshold, derived alike by every charter-holder, naming no vessel, stable across rotation. ''PRIOR ART: this is a KERI Autonomic Identifier'' — an AID is derived from an //inception event// that //MUST include the list of controlling public keys and a signature threshold//, exactly these inputs. The charter chain already cites KERI for pre-rotation and then names the island by a vessel key instead. ''The deny/allow asymmetry is the anti-flickering property'': a deny list is MONOTONE (once disallowed, never allowed) so it replicates without total consistency; an allow list has no such property. And CRDTs cannot enforce global invariants — a roster of who belongs IS one. ⚠ ''THE WIRING IS SMALLER THAN IT LOOKED — the browser already runs it.'' A browser vessel is a LEAF-NODE lararium (a herm carrying operator/user CRDT caps) and resolves `inviteNexusPubkey ?? relayGatePubKey ?? own DID`, documented as //the Nexus this vessel crosses into//. So composing a shared plane under a FOREIGN key stands WITNESSED (`node-who-face-parity`: //a node anchors its confederation, so its gate key IS the Nexus key its leaves pass back//). The node holds only the last term. ''The anchor model covers a FLEET and stops at a PEER RELATION'': when two sovereign operators contract, neither dials the other, so neither gate key names their island — and that is the slot the genesis AID fills. `nexusIdentity` now carries the whole chain (`explicit → charter genesis → anchor gate → own`), one rule both openers can share, with a charter OUTRANKING an anchor because it names a relation this vessel CONSENTED to where an anchor names a relay it merely reaches. Wakes on the operator's per-plane ruling (WHO share · ANTIGEN share = governance call · MEMBERS local), then the two openers pass the chain instead of their own key.
* ✓ ''RESTART BLINDNESS RESOLVES AS CORRECT — pinned, not fixed''. Residency state lives in ONE in-memory map and nothing persists it, so a restarted vessel holds no reading and every realm reads `unread` until something touches it. That names exactly what stands: after a boot this vessel genuinely has not looked. ''Persisting it would carry a verdict the vessel can no longer stand behind'' — the substrate may have moved while it was down, so a restored `dissolved` reports a stale departure and a restored `alive` vouches for a bag it never opened. The forgetting IS the guard, and a test now says so before someone "repairs" it.
* ⚠ ''OPERATOR-NESS SITS OUTSIDE THE CAP STACK — it is enforced by DISK ACCESS''. The cap doctrine holds that //vessel-kinds are different STACKS, never feature-flags//, and the three groups //compose by what a vessel HOLDS//. Operator power composes by nothing: seating a charter, contracting an operator, accepting carriage and flipping a posture all reach the SEAL HOME on disk through the node CLI, and only `nexus-refresh` / `nexus-rekey` / `nexus-reshare` stand as daemon verbs at all. ''A browser vessel reaches NO nexus/seal surface'' — so the operator/user seam is drawn by WHICH BINARY RUNS, never by a capability. ⚠ TWO CONSEQUENCES: (1) an operator's OWN browser device cannot exercise operator power — there is no door, which is the device-reach thread; (2) anything holding the seal-home path IS an operator regardless of caps, so the gate is the filesystem. ✓ ''It also SETTLES leaf-vs-anchor structurally'': a leaf holds no seal home, so it supplies an anchor and no genesis, and `nexusIdentity`'s charter-over-anchor ordering never gets to decide against it — pinned by test. ''The seam in one line'': an operator KEEPS a hearth and may declare where it stands; a user WALKS the road and is told which shrine they arrived at. Wakes when operator power needs to reach a browser (an operator-capped leaf), which is the moment the seam must become a CAP rather than a path.
* ⚠ ''CO-HOSTED SOVEREIGNTY IS CLAIMED, MEASURED, AND UNENFORCED — three spirits, 2026-08-31''. `causal-island#substrate-not-sovereignty` RULES it supported: //many sovereign vessels MAY run on one machine//, with a MAY-co-hold / MUST-stay-separate table — and ''`uid` appears in NEITHER column''. `rendezvous-path` designs for it explicitly (//the uid keeps two operators apart, the digest keeps two roots apart//), and `nexus-bilateral-genesis` + `contract-witness.sh` walk two roots under ONE uid. ⚠ But ''every isolation measurement is a destroys-nothing REHEARSAL claim, never a mutual-non-reachability claim between two LIVE peers''. `node-vessel-identity:43` stated the invariant as somebody else's job — //caller must ensure the identity dir is not world-readable// — and ✓ ''a caller now does'': `persistIdentityAnchors` creates the home 0700, reads the mode BACK, and warns when group or other bits stand open (`identityHomeClosure`). ⚠ It REPORTS and never repairs — a vessel widened on purpose has reasons, and a silent chmod would hide what an operator wants to read. ⚠ ''And it closes only the OTHER-USER half'': a same-uid neighbour reaches that home whatever the bits hold, which leaves the co-hosting gap exactly where it stood. The charter writes with NO mode (0644 under a standard umask); persona-root signing seeds sit cleartext at 0600, so ''a same-uid process reading a peer vessel's identity dir can forge that vessel's whole seal quorum''. `process.getuid()` appears 6 times, ALL path construction, never a comparison. And the docker harness isolates by MOUNT NAMESPACE, not uid — no `user:` directive stands in any compose file, so both containers run as root. ⚠ `vessel-caps:162` promises //no ambient authority … an unsigned or absent cap fails loud, never falls through to ambient trust (the confused-deputy guard, a standing covenant)// — ''a covenant the seal-home door does not stand under''.
* ''PRIOR ART RULES AGAINST THE PATH-AS-CREDENTIAL SHAPE, and names the cure''. Miller (//Robust Composition//, //Capability Myths Demolished//) puts an env-var-naming-a-root as textbook AMBIENT AUTHORITY, and the original confused-deputy example IS a program writing to a caller-designated PATH while holding its own authority. Capsicum exists to remove global-namespace lookup for exactly this reason. ⚠ ''BUT Capsicum BLESSES directory capabilities'' — a delegated namespace subset held as an unforgeable FD. The difference is total: an fd is HANDED to the process and has no path back to `/`; an env var is a STRING the process reads, forgeable by anything that can set the environment. ''A credential must be unforgeable, delegable, attenuable and revocable, and a path string is none of the four.'' Cure shape: pass an opened handle, drop ambient authority (`cap_enter`/`pledge`/Landlock), make the root UN-NAMEABLE rather than merely un-named; across machines that is a delegation token (UCAN / Meadowcap — asymmetric, since macaroon HMAC makes every verifier a forger).
* ⚠ ''"A DENY ONLY TIGHTENS" IS REFUTED BY FIELD EVIDENCE — this weakens the antigen-share case''. Fediverse blocklist research (tripleC; //A Blocklist is a Boundary//, CSCW 2025) measures four breakages: shared lists ''carry no selection criteria'', are ''binary'', ''grow only'', and inclusion is ''effectively permanent'' — //a ratchet that cannot un-ratchet is safe to ADD to and impossible to correct//. They ''sever THIRD PARTIES'' — a replicated entry at operator A removes reachability for A's USERS, who never joined A's threat model, so tightening at the operator layer WIDENS the blast radius at the user layer. And a widely-imported list ''becomes the global registry through the back door'', at the layer this architecture removed it from. ⚠ Matrix MSC2313 is the closest federated design (ban lists as ROOMS you subscribe to — the list replicates, ''the subscription stays local'', which matches our split) and its reviewers hit the unresolved problem: ''no common severity scale, so MERGING TWO LISTS IS UNDEFINED''. Let's-Revoke names the other trap: every pull-based scheme died of ''SOFT-FAIL'', and an offline-tolerant causal-island mesh is structurally a soft-fail environment. ''Mitigations the literature supports'': entries EXPIRE (a lease, never a ratchet) · ship PROVENANCE + criteria with every entry · define MERGE semantics before two lists exist. ''A quorum signature answers forgery and never legitimacy.''
* ✓ ''THE ANTIGEN SHARE IS ALREADY OPERATOR-RULED — stop holding it as a governance call''. `carry-contract:83` (Operator-ruled 2026-07-20): MANDATORY tier carries //@oracle + @crossroads (public + infra) + the Kapae antigen AT ALL TIMES//, and ''contracting into a Nexus IS agreeing to be an immune-propagation node''. What stands unruled is only the board's ADDRESS, never whether it propagates. ⚠ Note the mandatory tier does NOT name the members board.
* ⚠ ''ONE CHARTER PER VESSEL IS NOT A LAW — it is a guard bolted onto a SINGLE-SLOT FILE''. `seal-import` says //a charter arrives where none stands, or it refuses//, and its own remedy concedes multiplicity: //a partner's charter belongs BESIDE yours, never over it — point the import at a seal home this vessel did not found in//. What forces one-Nexus is PATH SHAPE: one `founding-roster.mem` at one unparameterized `larSealHome()`, and one `carriage-consent.json` beside it. ⚠ ''A scout called that consent file a cross-Nexus overwrite hazard and that reads FALSE'' — `carriageConsentPath` is scoped BY SEAL HOME, and one seal home holds one charter, so two Nexuses mean two seal homes and two consent files. The single-slot problem is `larSealHome()` itself, never the consent beside it. ''Multi-Nexus is CANON and TESTED'': `waking-floor:165` (//a vessel standing in more than one Nexus holds more than one window//) with a passing test (`waking-floor.test.ts:67-69`), `fleetSpan` measures cross-Nexus dyads as //the exit right, measured//, and a text //admitted at two Nexuses declares twice//. ''PRIOR ART HAS NO DEFENDER OF SINGLE-MEMBERSHIP'': Willow (a peer holds as many stores as it wants; communal vs owned namespaces), Keyhive (group management AS a CRDT, coordination-free revocation, documents reachable through overlapping groups), MLS (a client is routinely in many groups), Matrix (one room per homeserver was never considered). Nostr's relay-bound groups demonstrate the failure — ''FORKING on group id'' — that follows from binding group identity to a server. ⚠ The known cost of single-membership: an operator needing two Nexuses runs two vessels, and then either shares keys (fusing the trust domains they meant to separate) or holds two unrelated identities nobody can address as one. ''Willow/Keyhive answer: the NAMESPACE is the unit of membership, the KEY is the unit of identity, kept orthogonal.'' ⚠ AND THE TWO OPEN QUESTIONS ARE ONE: the only suggested workaround for a second charter is a second `LAR_ROOT`, which is exactly the co-hosting shape with no local-process boundary.
* ✓ ''THE SOURCE OF A TRANSFER WENT UNGATED — closed 2026-08-31, and the research re-graded the fix''. `action-handler` cap-checked the DESTINATION for every verb and the SOURCE for `MOVE` alone, so `ADD` and `COPY` read a source bag with the handler's own cross-plane reach while the caller held nothing on it. Nothing upstream covered it: `deriveRoutedCap` picks `bagUrl ?? toBag ?? dest ?? bag ?? targetBag` — the destination again — and the store declines to help (//read and write share the store: the doc carries no read-only flag, the cap-gate is the authority//). ⚠ ''A first fix graded the source `read` and the literature calls that too weak.'' In confidentiality terms the household plane holds the MORE confidential material and the public plane the less, so a grant outward RELAXES a policy — ''declassification'', the act Bell-LaPadula's *-property exists to forbid — and grading by destination grades the LOW side. DLM puts declassification authority with the principal who OWNS the source policy; robust declassification adds that the DECISION must carry integrity, which the destination cap supplies. ⚠ ''AND GRADING BOTH SIDES ALIKE BROKE THE INWARD PATH'' (operator, 2026-08-31): //a user should have the cap to copy and alter a public tiddler into a more private bag (a personal shadow copy). BUT only the kahu-cabal-signers may move things in the other direction — from any private to any public bag/doc//. ''The price follows the DIRECTION'', which reads as Bell-LaPadula arrived at from the other side: a write-UP (toward private) raises confinement and runs free; a write-DOWN (toward public) relaxes it and stands restricted. ✓ `crossingDirection` carries the rule — inward/lateral want `read` on the source, outward wants the source owner AND the ''kahu-cabal quorum'', since one hand cannot answer for an act nobody can walk back, and EVERY step up the ladder counts (a `contract`→`public` grant reaches the widest audience of all). ⚠ ''THE GATE CANNOT REACH IT YET'': telling the directions apart wants each bag's publicity TIER, and no bag→tier reader stands in `action-handler`. Interim: the source answers at `read` (strictly more than the nothing it answered before, and no cost on the shadow-copy path), `MOVE` keeps `admin` because it tombstones the source. ⚠ ''An OUTWARD crossing therefore passes on a read cap alone until a tier reader lands.'' Pinned by two tests. ⚠ ''AND THE READER SITS NEARER THAN THAT SOUNDS'': `resolveTierForDoc(capTiers, docId)` already stands, and `federation-gate` uses it. Its ring's ''`floor` oracle IS wired'' on the live path (reading the same federatable-set + seal oracles the carry-split consults, via `structuralFloorFor`), and with `declared` returning null the FLOOR governs — a real publicity tier, not a blank. What no caller builds is a ''`CapTierRing` at the vessel'': the string appears in `federation-gate` alone across node and browser. So lighting the direction gate wants (1) a ring constructed where the vessel stands and threaded to `action-handler`, and — for a DECLARED tier above the floor — (2) the recipe surface to seat a `capTier` datum plus a doc→bag resolver over the bag registry, which `federation-gate` names as its own unlit shore.
* ⚠ ''AND THE CROSSING RAIL INHERITS THREE MORE REQUIREMENTS — research, unbuilt''. ''(1) Both designations must arrive as CAPABILITIES presented by the caller'', never one cap plus a STRING naming the other — a rite holding ambient reach over the temple while the caller names the household bag stays the confused deputy however the grades read. ''(2) The batch reads as a SAGA whose FIRST outward grant is the PIVOT.'' Compensation does not exist here (no reverse crossing), so every validation of all N titles must land BEFORE the first grant, and everything after it must stay idempotent and forward-retried — a single-writer rail buys serialisation, never durability. A transfer-id gives traceability, not atomicity: it wants ''per-title idempotency keys'' (`H(transfer-id ‖ title)`) and a batch lifecycle (`staged → pivoted → complete`) so a half-landing can be NAMED rather than read as success. ''(3) The union catalog must declare its deletion semantics'' — OAI-PMH forces `deletedRecord: no|transient|persistent`, and since our reverse crossing does not exist ours reads `no`; the measured failure mode is that the NEGATIVE record gets dropped by a query written for the positive case, so deaccession propagation wants its own gate. ⚠ Grain mismatch bites too: a union catalog reconciling at BAG grain against emissions at TITLE grain reads the untransmitted titles as a deaccession.
* ✓ ''THE MESH GRID CLOSES — 18/18 cells, every scenario green ALONE; the full run flakes on the boot burr''. `nexus seal show` now reads QUORUM at the ruled shape (operator, 2026-08-31): ''two operators and FOUR personas'' — A seating three as the quorum seed, B carrying one in. `nexusPhase` counts PERSONAS at the top rung and RELATIONS at the middle, with `relations <= 0` branching FIRST so a founder holding four or nine chairs alone still reads SEED — chairs from one vault supply no hand that can refuse, and the ordering is what protects that. `lararium-c` stood briefly to reach quorum by relations and the ruling retired it: a quorum wants no third operator. ''Coverage reads 18 reachable · 18 claimed · 0 unreachable · 11 scenarios''; one declared GAP remains (realm feeding does not cross operators — design, not failure). ⚠ ''A back-to-back `all` run scores 58 ok / 2 red, and BOTH reds re-run green alone'' — //A exported no charter// and //the relay never answered//, which are the cold-found patience burr, never logic. ✓ ''RESOLVED — THE FULL GRID RUNS GREEN.'' The patience took its cold term (`HEARTH_READY_MS` 3s → 15s, `LAR_HEARTH_READY_MS` to move it) and three back-to-back `all` runs read ''58 ok / 2 red → 68 ok / 1 red → 73 ok / 0 red'', with ''no resolve-timeout anywhere in the clean run''. ⚠ ''So the flake belonged to the SYSTEM UNDER TEST, never to the harness'' — a cold-start budget measured for a warm read, surfaced by a run that cold-founds eleven times in sequence. The one remaining non-ok reports as the declared GAP it is.
* ⚠ ''TWO INSTRUMENT LIES MEASURED IN THE SAME WALK''. A realm doc id reads 64 HEX, and four ladder steps carried `o` and `r` — the verb refused at usage on every run while `mesh-coverage-witness` reported the cells WALKED. ''It reads COVERS claims, never passes'', and now says so in its own header: read it beside a green run, never instead of one. And a quorum read `multisig` for one whole walk because the CLI got rebuilt and `mesh` did not — ''the binary reads dist'', and `nexusPhase` lives in mesh.
* ⚠ ''THE LEDGER MOVE, COLLIDED AGAINST CANON — pre-authorised, and THREE constraints nobody had priced''. ✓ The move stands PRE-AUTHORISED with two conditions, and one already holds: `first-realm-arc` rules //what a maintenance roll must carry, WHEREVER the ledger sits// and declines to site it, so the daemon home reads INHERITED rather than ruled. ✓ ''The disclosure tier does NOT move'': `realm-glamour` already lists //liveness lease slots// members-only ''beside'' //substrate content// and the roster, so a content-holder is a cabal member by construction. ✓ And `cabal-realm#P4` ARGUES FOR it — //the maintenance edges stay observable to members, or capture hides// — which the capture-clock cannot deliver while the tally sits in private drawers.
** ⚠ ''(1) FORK the primitive, never RE-HOME it.'' `lamplighters:160` rules //the NEXUS fences, never the cabal — an epoch means nothing outside the island that rolls it//. A realm ledger is cross-operator BY DESIGN, so re-homing would leave two instances of one primitive carrying ''opposite scope laws'' under one `leaseEpochPrefix`. The fence stays in the daemon bag; the tally wants its own carrier.
** ⚠ ''(2) THE SLOT-MAP SHAPE ASSUMES A FIXED WRITER-SET.'' `epoch-binding-surfaces:15` names the slot-map for //where the writer-set is fixed//, against a hash-linked chain head //where the writer-set itself rotates//. A realm's dwellers ARRIVE (`hoʻokipa`), so by that meme's own criterion the realm ledger belongs on the chain-head shape. ''Nothing rules it for realms'', but the criterion stands stated and cuts against the current carrier.
** ⚠ ''(3) THE RESIDENCY CROSS-WIRE BAN GETS EASY TO TRIP.'' If slots live IN the substrate then a remote roll IS a change to the substrate doc, so any future //warm on doc-change// wiring would breach the standing ban silently. The likeliest accidental violation the move introduces.
** ⚠ ''AND THE HISTORY LEAKS FORWARD.'' `forward_secrecy` stays FALSE by deliberate threat-model choice, so a dweller admitted later syncs the ENTIRE historical tally — who fed, how deep, from the beginning. Nothing licenses or forbids that. ⚠ Canon also asserts the substrate sits behind Keyhive CGKA while ''the code attaches nothing'': `foundCabalRealm` only pairs a plain `substrateUrl` with the sentinel.
** ⚠ ''PLUMBING ABSENT'': `ResolveDaemonStore` takes NO realm argument, the only resolver is bag-URI-keyed through the catalog, and a realm's `substrateUrl` never registers there — `realm-feed` literally cannot address a realm substrate today. ''The seal half is built and tested''; the relocation half needs a doc→store path that does not exist.
** ⚠ ''EROSION STAYS BLOCKED, AND THE MOVE WORSENS IT.'' Canon EXPECTS decay (//stop dwelling and the depth erodes//), the idle-clock concession explicitly pins //do NOT build the three-state ahi kā erosion reading//, and a max-register on a SHARED doc makes a bad or stale-high roll permanent across every dweller's replica with no lowering act by ruling. Today such a slot stays contained to one private drawer. ''Fork-as-exit remains the only shrink primitive canon supplies.''
* ✓ ''STALE CLAIM CORRECTED IN SOURCE''. `cabal-realm.ts` still read //the instrument that CAN see the collective is `realmStanding` … those replicate, so other faces' offerings arrive// — retracted in this ledger after the docker walk and never fixed in the code. The comment now states what was measured: no instrument here sees the collective, and both readings answer for the vessel holding them.
* ⚠ ''`unavailable` READS TRANSIENT UPSTREAM — our resolver's premise stands contradicted at the source''. The boot resolver treats a hearth-private `unavailable` as TERMINAL (//no peer will ever carry it, so fail FAST on the signal//). automerge-repo documents the opposite: a handle //can be unavailable because we didn't have it in storage and no peers responded … but then another peer comes online … and so it transitions to READY//, and `DocumentQuery.sourcePending` carries //if the query was `unavailable`, it transitions back to `loading`//. ''Upstream issue #293 is titled "An unavailable document may just not be found yet"'' and a maintainer answers //should we remove the immediate unavailable event// with "yes"; #155 records that `find()` at boot //will ALWAYS create a handle which is unavailable// while adapters connect. ⚠ MEASURED HERE: a hearth died on `reason: 'doc-unavailable'` for the doc id it had just seeded, and ''raising the deadline could not touch it because the terminal path never reaches the deadline''.
* ⚠ ''`allowableStates` HAS NO EFFECT — VERIFIED IN OUR OWN PINNED COPY''. `Repo.d.ts` in `2.6.0-alpha.3` marks it `@deprecated This no longer has any effect, instead you should use findWithProgress`. Three carriers named it as a live strategy (`open-vessel-core`, `base-doc`, `who-face`) and now name `findWithProgress` instead. ⚠ ''The recall entry "repo.find() rejects on unavailable → allowableStates" reads STALE'' and wants re-writing.
* ''THE BETTER CURE, from the sources'': ''(1) RETAIN THE HANDLE `create()` returned'' — within one Repo, find-after-create returns the SAME query and cannot read unavailable, so ''seeing `unavailable` proves the read is NOT hitting the repo that wrote it''. Re-resolving by URL //buys nothing and adds a state machine that can reject//. ''(2) `await repo.flush()` BEFORE publishing the pointer'' — saves ride an `asyncThrottle` at `saveDebounceRate = 100`, so a write sits off disk when `create()` returns, and a pointer file lands synchronously while its payload does not (the same split #266 reports as the actual hazard). ''(3) READ THE SOURCES MAP, never the collapsed verdict'' — `findWithProgress().peek()` returns `{state, sources}`, which separates //storage answered: nothing here// from //settled with zero network sources attached//; the second reads vacuous, and on an idle machine it is the one we hit. ⚠ ''(4) CHECK THE SEED SHAPE'': `create({})` with an empty object does not sync (upstream #488/#538, fix unmerged into our alpha) and produces exactly //Document … is unavailable// for a doc that demonstrably exists. ⚠ ''(5) DO NOT RACE WASM INIT AGAINST A RESOLVE DEADLINE'' — start the storage load first and take the deadline from the storage promise alone.
* ⚠ ''THE GRACE DID NOT CLOSE IT EITHER — SIX SAMPLES, and the flake reads ~50/50 regardless''. `all` runs: 3s → 58/2 · 15s → 68/1 · 15s → ''73/0'' · 15s → 68/2 · 15s+grace → ''73/0'' · 15s+grace → 68/1. ''Neither the budget nor the grace moves it'', and two clean runs sat beside two dirty ones under identical code — ''one clean sample proves a run, never a fix'', measured twice over.
* ⚠ ''A DURABILITY NARROWING THAT RETRACTS ITSELF — read this whole entry before acting on it''. `social-seed` does `repo.create(emptyLarDoc())` + `handle.change(...)` and returns the handle — ''no flush''. Saves ride an `asyncThrottle` at `saveDebounceRate = 100`, so a write sits OFF DISK when `create()` returns, ⚠ ''A DRAFT CLAIMED no `repo.flush()` stands in the founding surface, AND THAT READS FALSE'' — the grep scoped `lares-cli/src` and `lararium-keyhive/src` while the founding runs in `lararium-node/src/commands/init.ts`, which flushes at three sites, ''on the SAME repo that seeds the doc'' (`foundThePlace({ repo, … })`). ''So the exit-inside-the-throttle candidate stands REFUTED'' and the flake's root cause reads UNPROVEN again. The catalog pointer already gets this right — //the flush comes FIRST. A pointer written after its referent is durable can be stale; one written before can be a lie// — and ''the daemon-doc path never learned it''. ✓ ''WHAT DOES STAND: the founding and the read sit in TWO PROCESSES.'' `lararium-container-boot.sh` runs `lares vessel found` (line 26) and then `exec … main.js` (line 115), so the seeding repo and the reading repo differ BY CONSTRUCTION — which rules out the upstream comfort that a find-after-create returns the same query. ✓ ''AND ONE REAL ORDERING DEFECT, FIXED'': `init.ts` wrote the social-bootstrap pointer BEFORE `await repo.flush()`, inverting the rule the catalog pointer states three files away — //a pointer written after its referent is durable can be stale; one written before can be a LIE//. A stop between the two would leave a pointer naming a doc no later boot can find. The flush now comes first. ⚠ ''This is a latent-crash fix and NOT a proven cure for the flake'', since the flush did complete on every observed run. ''Do not widen another number, and do not claim a cure, until a dirty run is caught with evidence about WHICH source answered.''
* ✓ ''INTERIM LANDED — a grace, opt-in.'' `unavailableVerdict` gives a hearth-private absence a 2s grace ONLY where the caller passes `expectPresent` (the two `@daemon` reads, which hold the url from a bootstrap). A caller that MINTS on absence — `materializeSharedLarDoc` — pays nothing, since absence rides its ordinary path; a first draft charged it the grace and slowed every blank-board mint into a test timeout. ''This buys a flush its moment; it does not implement (1)–(3), which remain the real cure.''
* ✓ ''INK & SWITCH DEPENDENCY SCOUT — 2026-09-01. The upgrade reads as a NON-QUESTION for the package that matters''. `@automerge/automerge-repo` 2.6.0-alpha.3 ''IS the newest `latest`'' — no 2.6.0 final exists, no 2.7 or 3.x line stands, and `main` sits 5 commits ahead touching ''zero files under `packages/automerge-repo/src`''. ⚠ ''NOTHING UPSTREAM FIXES OUR `unavailable` BUG'': #293 · #155 · #208 · #126 · #488 all OPEN (some untouched for three years), #538 CLOSED AS A DUPLICATE rather than fixed, and ''#631 already sits as an ancestor of our pin'' — we hold that fix. ''Do not spend an upgrade on this.''
** ✓ `@automerge/automerge` 3.4.0 → ''3.4.1'' reads low-risk: 53 commits of deps/CI plus hexane hardening, inside repo's own `^3.2.6` range. The heavy landing (hexane 1.0.0-alpha4, zlib-rs, a bool-column CORRUPTION fix) rode 3.4.0, which we already run.
** ⚠ `@keyhive/keyhive` alpha.6 → alpha.8 carries ''capability-escalation fixes'' worth having (transitive authority, //limit delegation to weakest link//, refusing a forged delegation, keeping relay members out of the CGKA tree, losing CGKA access through a revoked group) — but ''#213 replaces BeeKEM key ratcheting with independently sampled secrets'', so alpha.6 and alpha.8 peers ''will not interoperate''. ''A coordinated flag-day across the mesh, never a lockfile edit.'' ✓ ''THE FLAG-DAY LANDED 2026-09-01'': both pins read `0.1.0-alpha.8`, the lockfile resolves ''only'' alpha.8, and keyhive 41/41 · mesh 2086 · node 818 · typecheck clean. ⚠ ''Every vessel image MUST rebuild together'' — a container still carrying alpha.6 will not key with one that moved, and the tests cannot see that because they move as one.
** ⚠ ''`@keyhive/keyhive`'s npm `latest` TAG POINTS AT A YEAR-OLD PLACEHOLDER'' (`0.0.0-alpha.0`, 2025-09-22); the live line rides `next`. ✓ Our two pins read EXACT and the lockfile resolves only `0.1.0-alpha.6`, so nothing bites today — ''any loose add anywhere silently takes the placeholder''.
** ⚠ ''TWO SIDE LINES READ AS TRAPS'': `subduction` moves (48 releases, tracking PR #601) but ''downgrades `@automerge/automerge` to a pinned 3.3.2'' and swaps the sync protocol wholesale — an evaluation project. `authors` has not moved since 2026-07-03 and its `2.7.0-` version ''does NOT mean a 2.7 line exists''. ''WATCH, never track'', PR #725 `improve-adapter-contract`: it adds exactly the distinction this bug needs (`DocumentUnavailable` vs `DocumentLoadFailed` — //I don't have it// against //I could not check//) and stands unmerged, 5 behind main.
* ⚠ ''THE FLAKE'S MECHANISM, READ FROM UPSTREAM SOURCE — and the cure sits on OUR side''. `StorageSource.attach()` on the create path fires the throttled save and ''IMMEDIATELY calls `query.sourceUnavailable("storage")`''; `DocSynchronizer.#updateAvailability()` reports `sourceUnavailable("automerge-sync")` when ''`peers.size === 0`''; `DocumentQuery.#computeState()` reads ready only when `handle.heads().length > 0`, and otherwise — ''all sources settled, none pending'' — returns `unavailable`. ''On a cold start with no peers and a write still in the 100ms throttle, `unavailable` arrives immediately and BY DESIGN.'' ✓ ''alpha.3 ALREADY exposes the per-source map'' (`sources: Record<string, SourceState>` via `peek()`), so ''we can separate //storage answered nothing// from //settled with zero peers// TODAY'' without waiting on #725. ⚠ And `Repo.shutdown()` reads BEST-EFFORT since #719 — it swallows flush failures, and its own comment says call `flush()` explicitly first.
* ⚠ ''ONE CARRIER FAILS THE CORPUS ROUND-TRIP — PRE-EXISTING, and it wants an owner''. `lararium-tw5` `tests/grammar-in-a-wiki.test.ts` reports ''`bags/elyncia/…/primary-characters/lady-aki.mem: body does not render back''`. ✓ ''Proven not to be a build gap and not a regression'': it survives a full `lararium-tw5` rebuild, and against the pre-session tree the ''carrier is byte-identical and every parser source matches''. The whole rest of the corpus (>500 carriers) renders back. ''One carrier, one shape — read it before trusting any corpus-wide render claim.''
* ⚠ ''THE E2E HARNESS HAS ROTTED, AND NOTHING RAN IT — 18 failed / 41 passed, 9 of 15 files (2026-09-01)''. `tests/` declares `test:e2e`, ''not'' `test`, so `pnpm -r test` skips it BY DESIGN and no witness carries it: the unit suites stayed green at 3,979 while this stood red. ✓ ''NOT caused by the dep bump'' — the one bumped dep that could plausibly touch decomposition (`smol-toml` 1.7.1→1.8.0, the meta-block parser) was reverted and the failure reproduced BYTE-IDENTICAL; the rest are crypto hardening and test tooling, which cannot produce a bag-naming mismatch. ⚠ ''It is DRIFT'': `e2e/smoke.test.ts` last moved 2026-08-23 while `lares-cli/src` moved through 2026-08-31 — 44 files and ~1,574 insertions across cli+tw5 with no harness update. Two measured shapes: ''`expected 22 to be 20`'' (boot-meme LOAD emits more records than parent+ahu) and ''`bags/@grove` expected, `bags/grove` received'' (the bare-@ migration never reached the harness). ''Fix the assertions to the current model, then give the harness a witness'' — an instrument nobody runs decays exactly like a comment.
* ⏸ ''HELD, NOT ACTED ON (operator, 2026-09-02) — a LARES STARTUP MODULE inside the VM.'' The shape: our own `module-type: startup` tiddler that always runs in the VM ''after'' the plugin + tiddlers get custom-injected at startup, wiring whatever the projection environment lacks. ✓ ''The seam already stands and is used twice'' — `capture-annotate-vm.ts` and `grammar-cache.ts` both ship as `module-type: startup`, so this is an established pattern rather than a new mechanism. ''Do not fire it yet'': a research spirit is out on off-main-thread engine prior art (WorkerDOM, remote-dom, LiveView/Blazor, partytown), deliberately WITHOUT being told what is broken here, so its findings arrive uncontaminated. Read those first — the field may name the class, and a cure chosen before the reading would be the conclusion-first move this house keeps catching.
* tombstone/whiteout verb — reserved (§3); wakes when layered delete needs it
* Automerge history growth — DXOS-epoch pattern watched (§1); wakes on real growth; the §7 bus-doc law = a second consumer
* `lares wiki` subcommands print human-only; `wiki open` selects-for-next-boot only
* engine-swap reconcile answered "current" against a stale engine — epoch-design shore
* e2e trends long (reboot vector 73s) — split a slow lane when the suite hurts
* NFC assertion at the shore — rides the INGEST gesture (spec already pins NFC)
* parser edge-field plane still implements the retired typed-edge law (edge-out-<family>-<role> emission; edge.ts filter; kumu-device.ts as sole live consumer) — recut BEFORE or WITH INGEST's parser work (witness once, not twice)
* module gate positive-path vector absent (bootTrustedModules via stack:has witnessed only indirectly) — rides the next lararium-tw5 touch
* smoke-plugin-boot.ts fails on surfaces lagging the plugin (kau templates, parser registration) — pre-existing; wakes when plugin smoke matters or at the next plugin recut
* TimeoutNegativeWarning (-15/-26) in e2e runs — pre-existing, un-localized (no computed setTimeout in src); wakes on next firing under node --trace-warnings
* island→admin verb plane still rides the postMessage switchboard — retires onto record pairs per §7's lane law; the durable mailbox (vessel-mailbox.ts, keel-resident, drains on ea) already carries admin→island. ORDERING CRUCIBLE-PASSED 2026-06-12 (moʻolelo in hoike below): LOAD stands as the FIRST witness of verb-pair physics; INGEST brings the SECOND witness + the shore forge (three-way diff, replace-by-group, quiescence, NFC); the wire compiler rides INGEST's organs; then residency (vector 2) → closure (3); the switchboard retires on two-witness verb-pair INTEGRITY, the platform-free handlers (verb-dispatcher/summons/vm, action-handler, wiki-compose/draft/mint) migrate in-VM during that recut (one witness not two), hull steps 2–4 follow; ingest-gate packing decided at the island-verb build

